ices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+4y=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-94268" "field" "selec