"/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-64250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "4\\pi \\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "\\pi \\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\pi \\,\\text{s}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-94271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "\\pi \\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\pi \\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}\\pi \\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-79487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-79487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-79487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-79487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-79487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-79487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{5}t+B\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\sqrt{5}t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=5\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{5}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{5}t$\n\nTherefore, only statement II could be used to represent $y(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-94278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/s