orname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-64252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R{sin}^{2}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=49\\,⟹\\,\\omega =7.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t$\n\nTherefore, only function I could be used to represent  $y(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-94277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡16t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡16t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and II could be used to represent $y(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-94276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2\\sqrt{2}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=2\\sqrt{2}R\\,⟹\\,R=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-94283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+64y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=64\\,⟹\\,\\omega =8.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8R\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=8R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-64253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "2\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "4\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=144\\,⟹\\,\\omega =12.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=12R\\operatorname{cos}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=12R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-64254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-79493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $6\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-79493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $6\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=6.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3R\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=6$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n6=3R\\,⟹\\,R=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $2\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-79493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-79493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-79493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "6\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-79493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5R\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=5R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-94280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=21\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+3y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ),$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{21}{7}=3\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\sqrt{3}$ and ${ϕ}_{1}=ϕ$ in form 2 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-94288" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94288" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94288" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $32\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{8}{32}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-64258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I  only"] ["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $16\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=4\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-94285" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94285" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+2y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+4y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+8y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-94285" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=32\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{32}{8}=4.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-64255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+2y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+4y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}-4y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{2}y=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+4y=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{12}=2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "370351b4133d4ba5220f8044d94246ad417420f7548e3be392bed7d50887d919"} {"question" "q-64229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+2y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+4y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+\\sqrt{2}y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}-4y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}-\\sqrt{2}y=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "q-64229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+2y=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/state.json" "file_sha256" "d34deaaaac5100f9d2170d30e24e7b9b26f4fa8a6ba8c769fe9c56391c8c8785"} {"question" "e-6619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\n- $y'' + 81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' - 81y = 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/example-6619.json" "file_sha256" "6ab8a762902c56bee365e42db386cefac3e01fa9eb89be362d4bc62f0e591585"} {"question" "e-6619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I takes the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I represents SHM.\n\n- Equation II does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nbecause it has a $y'$ term.\n\n- Equation III does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt would require $\\omega^2 = -81,$ which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/example-6619.json" "file_sha256" "6ab8a762902c56bee365e42db386cefac3e01fa9eb89be362d4bc62f0e591585"} {"question" "e-6620" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 100y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/example-6620.json" "file_sha256" "e5480bd4337c4ef5e3e13e5f71c46fd2e19bba0a3747ead5c901e472379ea9ac"} {"question" "e-6620" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10,\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/example-6620.json" "file_sha256" "e5480bd4337c4ef5e3e13e5f71c46fd2e19bba0a3747ead5c901e472379ea9ac"} {"question" "e-6621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 9 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n\n- $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n\n- $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/example-6621.json" "file_sha256" "fd38a4b00d63a60ea0cfd5c0c36b0b73d4d5daa91680ac7ebc2857b19e1cf748"} {"question" "e-6621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/example-6621.json" "file_sha256" "fd38a4b00d63a60ea0cfd5c0c36b0b73d4d5daa91680ac7ebc2857b19e1cf748"} {"question" "e-6622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 36y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\textrm{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/example-6622.json" "file_sha256" "68bd494d277948d8f4ceb01efc0fc08d7b7b6296bf612a4f04ead45b54b300f9"} {"question" "e-6622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0,\n$$\n\nand the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)},\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 36\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 6,\n$$\n\nso the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(6t+\\phi)}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0) = 0.$ Substituting this into the equation above gives\n\n$$\nR\\sin\\phi = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\phi = 0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\phi=0,$ our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{6 t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that $y'(0) = 3.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = 6R\\cos{6t},\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(0) = 3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3 = 6R\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad R=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\textrm{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/example-6622.json" "file_sha256" "68bd494d277948d8f4ceb01efc0fc08d7b7b6296bf612a4f04ead45b54b300f9"} {"question" "e-6623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)},$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/example-6623.json" "file_sha256" "0138b6be48a7136d53f477f587f757f62261e8ebe23063ac7f2d4dc01f26d21e"} {"question" "e-6623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m=36$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k=9$ has\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m} = \\dfrac{9}{36} = \\dfrac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nso its equation of motion is \n\n$$\ny'' + \\dfrac{1}{4}y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is different from the equation in statement I.\n\n- Statement II is true. The frequency $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi} \\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\\,R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1}),\\,R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $\\omega = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\phi_1 = \\phi$ in the form $R\\sin{(\\omega t+\\phi_2)}$ gives\n\n$$\ny(t) = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064220/example-6623.json" "file_sha256" "0138b6be48a7136d53f477f587f757f62261e8ebe23063ac7f2d4dc01f26d21e"}] "usage" {"q-64221" [] "e-6619" [] "e-6621" [] "q-64254" [] "e-6623" [] "q-64217" [] "e-6620" [] "e-6622" [] "q-64229" []} "basis" 2099 "retractions" [[17592186148077 :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?"] [17592186148079 :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 16y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?"] [17592186148088 :question/problem "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 144y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8m/s$, find the amplitude of the oscillation."] [17592186148081 :question/problem "A particle of mass $12kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 24kg/s^{2}$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?"] [17592186147288 :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\n1. $y'' + 81y = 0$\n2. $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n3. $y'' - 81y = 0$"] [17592186147288 :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I takes the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nwith\n$\\omega^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 9$.\nTherefore, equation I represents SHM.\n- Equation II does not take the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nbecause it has a $y'$ term.\n- Equation III does not take the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$.\nIt would require $\\omega^2 = -81$, which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186147289 :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 100y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?"] [17592186147289 :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$"] [17592186147290 :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 9 y = 0$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n2. $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n3. $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants."] [17592186147290 :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n3. $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n3. $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t)$."] [17592186147291 :question/problem "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 36y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$, find the amplitude of the oscillation."] [17592186147291 :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nand the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)}\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 36\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 6\n$$\n\nso the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(6t+\\phi)}\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0) = 0$. Substituting this into the equation above gives\n\n$$\nR\\sin\\phi = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\phi = 0\n$$\n\nSince $\\phi=0$, our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{6 t}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0$, which means that $y'(0) = 3$. Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = 6R\\cos{6t}\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(0) = 3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3 = 6R\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad R=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$"] [17592186147292 :question/problem "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.\n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n2. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n3. The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}$, where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase."] [17592186147292 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m=36$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k=9$ has\n$\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m} = \\dfrac{9}{36} = \\dfrac{1}{4}$,\nso its equation of motion is\n$y'' + \\dfrac{1}{4}y = 0$,\nwhich is different from the equation in statement I.\n- Statement II is true. The frequency $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) is given by\n$f = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi} \\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- Statement III is true. The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms:\n$A\\cos ωt + B\\sin ωt, R\\cos (ωt + ϕ_{1}), R\\sin (ωt + ϕ_{2})$.\nWriting $\\omega = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\phi_1 = \\phi$ in the form $R\\sin{(\\omega t+\\phi_2)}$ gives\n$y(t) = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186146474 17592186146475 17592186146476 17592186146477 17592186146478 17592186146486 17592186146487 17592186146488 17592186146489 17592186146490 17592186146498 17592186146499 17592186146500 17592186146501 17592186146502 17592186146540 17592186146541 17592186146542 17592186146543 17592186146544] "immutable_fields" [[17592186146473 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146473 :answer-field/id] [17592186146473 :answer-field/key] [17592186146473 :answer-field/type] [17592186146485 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146485 :answer-field/id] [17592186146485 :answer-field/key] [17592186146485 :answer-field/type] [17592186146497 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146497 :answer-field/id] [17592186146497 :answer-field/key] [17592186146497 :answer-field/type] [17592186146539 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146539 :answer-field/id] [17592186146539 :answer-field/key] [17592186146539 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
